ns. Maybe the user has a hand with multiple pungs. The existing pung of T4s. If the user can form another pung, that would help. But the hand doesn't have three of any other tile. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is aiming for a hand with All Chows. But the hand has a pung and some chows. \n\nThis is getting complicated. Let's think about which tile to discard to maximize future options. The user needs to discard one tile. The valid actions are Play B2, Play W8, Play F4, Play J3, Play B3, Play T6, Play T9, Play T3, Play B5, Play B4, Play T1. \n\nLooking at each tile:\n\n- B2: If discarded, the user loses the chance to form a chow with B2-B3-B4. \n\n- B3: Similarly, part of the B2-B3-B4 chow. \n\n- B4: Also part of that chow. \n\n- B5: If discarded, maybe the user can't form a B4-B5-B6 chow (but B6 isn't present). \n\n- T1, T3, T6, T9: Terminals. Discarding a terminal might be safe if the user isn't relying on them. \n\n- W8: A single tile. If discarded, it's not part of any chow. \n\n- F4 and J3: Honor tiles. If the user is aiming for All Honors, but they have other suited tiles. \n\nThe best tile to discard would be one that is not essential for potential melds. If the user can form a chow with B2-B3-B4, then keeping those is good. So discarding B5 might be better than discarding B2, B3, or B4. Similarly, W8 is a single tile that doesn't contribute to any meld, so discarding it would be safe. \n\nAlternatively, if the user wants to keep the B2-B3-B4 chow, they should not discard any of those. So the options are to discard W8, F4, J3, T6, T9, T3, T1, B5. \n\nBetween these, W8 is the least useful. Discarding W8 would allow the user to keep the B2-B3-B4 chow and possibly form another meld. \n\nAnother consideration is the game state. The unshown tiles list shows that W8 has 2 remaining. Discarding W8 might be safe if there are still some left. \n\nSo the best move is to discard W8. That would leave the user with B2, B3, B4 to form a chow, and other tiles that might form another meld or pair. \n\nTherefore, the answer is to play W8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has a pung of T4 and potential chows in B2-B3-B4. Discarding W8 (a non-contributing tile) preserves key meld opportunities and reduces risk.\nReason: W8 is a single tile with no chow potential and is less critical than keeping B2/B3/B4 for a chow. Discarding it maintains flexibility for forming additional melds and avoids blocking key patterns.\nAnswer: Play W8"}
